      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine the maximum possible number of planes that can be determined by 12 points in space, we need to consider the number of ways to choose 3 points out of the 12. This is a combination problem where the order of selection does not matter. The number of ways to choose 3 points out of 12 is given by the binomial coefficient \\(\\binom{12}{3}\\).\n\nThe binomial coefficient \\(\\binom{n}{r}\\) is calculated using the formula:\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{r} = \\frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}\n\\]\nFor our problem, \\(n = 12\\) and \\(r = 3\\). Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12!}{3!(12-3)!} = \\frac{12!}{3! \\cdot 9!}\n\\]\nWe can simplify this by canceling out the \\(9!\\) in the numerator and the denominator:\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10 \\times 9!}{3! \\times 9!} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{3!}\n\\]\nNext, we calculate \\(3!\\):\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n\\]\nSo the expression simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{6} = \\frac{1320}{6} = 220\n\\]\nTherefore, the maximum possible number of planes that can be determined by 12 points in space is \\(\\boxed{220}\\).",
