      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the maximum possible number of planes that can be formed by 12 points in space, we need to consider that each plane is uniquely determined by any three non-collinear points. Therefore, the maximum number of planes is given by the number of ways to choose 3 points out of 12. This is a combination problem, specifically \\(\\binom{12}{3}\\).\n\nThe formula for combinations is:\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{r} = \\frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}\n\\]\nwhere \\(n\\) is the total number of items to choose from, \\(r\\) is the number of items to choose, and \\(!\\) denotes factorial, which is the product of all positive integers up to that number.\n\nIn this problem, \\(n = 12\\) and \\(r = 3\\). Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12!}{3!(12-3)!} = \\frac{12!}{3! \\cdot 9!}\n\\]\nSince \\(12! = 12 \\times 11 \\times 10 \\times 9!\\), the \\(9!\\) terms cancel out:\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{3!} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = \\frac{1320}{6} = 220\n\\]\nTherefore, the maximum possible number of planes that can be determined by 12 points in space is \\(\\boxed{220}\\).",
