      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the maximum possible number of planes that can be formed by 12 points in space, we need to consider the combinatorial nature of selecting three points out of the 12 points. The number of ways to choose 3 points out of 12 is given by the combination formula \\(\\binom{n}{r}\\), which represents the number of ways to choose \\(r\\) elements from a set of \\(n\\) elements without regard to the order of selection. The formula for combinations is:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{r} = \\frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}\n\\]\n\nHere, \\(n = 12\\) and \\(r = 3\\). Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12!}{3!(12-3)!} = \\frac{12!}{3! \\cdot 9!}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify this by canceling out the \\(9!\\) in the numerator and the denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10 \\times 9!}{3! \\times 9!} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{3!} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = \\frac{1320}{6} = 220\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the maximum possible number of planes that can be determined by 12 points in space is \\(\\boxed{220}\\).",
